WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.338 --> 00:00:01.171
- What's up, Believe Nation?

00:00:01.171 --> 00:00:03.622
My name is Evan Carmichael,
my one word is believe,

00:00:03.622 --> 00:00:05.253
and I believe in people

00:00:05.253 --> 00:00:06.861
more than they believe in themselves.

00:00:06.861 --> 00:00:09.361
And my sincere hope is
that if you see in you

00:00:09.361 --> 00:00:13.223
what I see in you, you'll go
off and change the planet.

00:00:13.223 --> 00:00:16.264
This is a new video today,
one we haven't done before.

00:00:16.264 --> 00:00:18.509
It's called How Billionaires Think:

00:00:18.509 --> 00:00:20.727
Success, Advice From the Top.

00:00:20.727 --> 00:00:22.167
There's some key messages here that I hope

00:00:22.167 --> 00:00:24.425
you'll take with you to
start seeing the world

00:00:24.425 --> 00:00:26.216
in a little different way,

00:00:26.216 --> 00:00:27.905
and some of that rubs off onto you

00:00:27.905 --> 00:00:30.211
to help you go off and do amazing things.

00:00:30.211 --> 00:00:31.543
Enjoy.

00:00:31.543 --> 00:00:34.210
(swooshy music)

00:00:40.322 --> 00:00:43.387
- I advise students, as much as possible,

00:00:43.387 --> 00:00:46.992
look for the job you would
take if you didn't need a job.

00:00:46.992 --> 00:00:49.043
I mean, don't sleepwalk through life

00:00:49.043 --> 00:00:52.133
and don't say it's all
going to be great, you know,

00:00:52.133 --> 00:00:54.315
I'll do this and I'll
do that and, you know,

00:00:54.315 --> 00:00:56.097
I'm just marking time
till I get to be older.

00:00:56.097 --> 00:00:57.990
I've told people, that's
like saving up sex

00:00:57.990 --> 00:00:59.222
for your old age,

00:00:59.222 --> 00:01:02.366
I mean, it's just, it is not a good idea.

00:01:02.366 --> 00:01:03.793
(laughter)

00:01:03.793 --> 00:01:05.210
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:06.121 --> 00:01:08.371
(clapping)

00:01:10.974 --> 00:01:13.514
- What are you urging them to do?

00:01:13.514 --> 00:01:15.014
- I'll explain it.

00:01:16.895 --> 00:01:18.140
Here's what I'm talking about.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:20.390
(laughter)

00:01:24.132 --> 00:01:27.410
You really want to be
doing what you love doing

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:30.119
and you can't necessarily
find it on your first job.

00:01:30.119 --> 00:01:30.952
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:01:30.952 --> 00:01:34.202
- But don't give up before you find it.

00:01:38.831 --> 00:01:40.954
- First of all, I really
sort of of thought, like,

00:01:40.954 --> 00:01:43.814
how can I provide advice
that would be most helpful

00:01:43.814 --> 00:01:47.483
and I'm not sure I've given
enough thought to give you

00:01:47.483 --> 00:01:51.566
the best possible answer,
but I think, certainly,

00:01:52.449 --> 00:01:55.961
being focused on something
that you're confident

00:01:55.961 --> 00:01:59.144
will have high value to someone else

00:01:59.144 --> 00:02:03.314
and just being really rigorous
in making that assessment,

00:02:03.314 --> 00:02:05.516
because people tend to,

00:02:05.516 --> 00:02:09.334
natural human tendency
is wishful thinking.

00:02:09.334 --> 00:02:11.727
So, a challenge for
entrepreneurs is to say,

00:02:11.727 --> 00:02:13.390
well what is the difference between

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:17.333
really believing in your
ideals and sticking to them

00:02:17.333 --> 00:02:20.334
versus pursuing some unrealistic dream

00:02:20.334 --> 00:02:21.916
that doesn't actually have merit.

00:02:21.916 --> 00:02:22.971
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:02:22.971 --> 00:02:25.544
- That is a really difficult thing to,

00:02:25.544 --> 00:02:27.469
can you tell the difference
between those two things.

00:02:27.469 --> 00:02:28.302
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:02:28.302 --> 00:02:29.286
- So, you need to be sort of,

00:02:29.286 --> 00:02:32.286
very rigorous in your self-analysis.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.953
I think certainly extremely tenacious,

00:02:36.953 --> 00:02:40.655
and then just work like hell, I mean,

00:02:40.655 --> 00:02:42.826
you just have to put in, you know,

00:02:42.826 --> 00:02:45.326
80-100 hour weeks, every week.

00:02:49.687 --> 00:02:50.624
- That's a lot of work.

00:02:50.624 --> 00:02:52.901
- And then, all those things
improve the odds of success.

00:02:52.901 --> 00:02:54.817
- [Interviewer] OK, great.

00:02:54.817 --> 00:02:57.670
- If other people are
putting in 40 hour work weeks

00:02:57.670 --> 00:02:59.956
and you're putting in 100 hour work weeks,

00:02:59.956 --> 00:03:04.058
then, even if you're doing the same thing,

00:03:04.058 --> 00:03:08.225
you know that in one year, you
will achieve in four months

00:03:10.493 --> 00:03:13.576
what it takes them a year to achieve.

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:21.536
- You just have to try different things

00:03:21.536 --> 00:03:24.369
and even if 99% of them fail,

00:03:25.799 --> 00:03:27.942
you only have to be right one time.

00:03:27.942 --> 00:03:30.650
You don't have to figure
it all out in advance.

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:33.639
You can be wrong, you can
pick the wrong career,

00:03:33.639 --> 00:03:35.176
or you can pick the wrong job,

00:03:35.176 --> 00:03:37.073
you can pick the wrong spouse,

00:03:37.073 --> 00:03:39.254
you can pick the wrong whatever,

00:03:39.254 --> 00:03:42.644
but you get it right one time, you're set.

00:03:42.644 --> 00:03:44.051
But, if you don't try, and if you don't

00:03:44.051 --> 00:03:45.657
go out there and try all
those different things

00:03:45.657 --> 00:03:47.297
you'll never get that one time.

00:03:47.297 --> 00:03:49.986
And so, it's not a question of, OK,

00:03:49.986 --> 00:03:51.623
how much different stuff do I do,

00:03:51.623 --> 00:03:53.188
or how many different
things should you try?

00:03:53.188 --> 00:03:54.773
Try them all.

00:03:54.773 --> 00:03:56.816
Try them all till you
find the one you like.

00:03:56.816 --> 00:03:59.156
And then it's like, it gets real easy,

00:03:59.156 --> 00:04:02.162
'cause even when (omitted) (omitted) up,

00:04:02.162 --> 00:04:05.134
you're on a mission, you're
loving what you do, you know.

00:04:05.134 --> 00:04:07.745
And then it gets easy, and
so that's just the approach

00:04:07.745 --> 00:04:08.984
I've always tried to instill

00:04:08.984 --> 00:04:12.151
in people who work for me, and myself.

00:04:18.407 --> 00:04:21.456
- So, it's easy to have ideas,

00:04:21.456 --> 00:04:25.487
it's very hard to turn an idea
into a successful product.

00:04:25.487 --> 00:04:27.062
There are a lot of steps in between,

00:04:27.062 --> 00:04:30.517
and it takes persistence, relentlessness.

00:04:30.517 --> 00:04:33.146
So, I always tell people
who are, you know,

00:04:33.146 --> 00:04:35.722
who think they want to be entrepreneurs,

00:04:35.722 --> 00:04:39.889
you need a combination of
stubborn relentlessness,

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:46.461
and flexibility, and you have
to know when to be which.

00:04:46.461 --> 00:04:49.173
And basically, you need to
be stubborn on your vision,

00:04:49.173 --> 00:04:52.173
because otherwise it'll
be too easy to give up,

00:04:52.173 --> 00:04:55.760
but you need to be very
flexible on the details

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:58.352
because as you go along
pursuing your vision,

00:04:58.352 --> 00:05:01.097
you'll find that some of your
preconceptions were wrong.

00:05:01.097 --> 00:05:03.879
And you're going to need to be
able to change those things.

00:05:03.879 --> 00:05:07.674
So, I think taking an idea successfully

00:05:07.674 --> 00:05:10.024
all the way to the market
and turning it into

00:05:10.024 --> 00:05:11.823
a real product that people care about

00:05:11.823 --> 00:05:14.243
and that really improves people's lives

00:05:14.243 --> 00:05:16.076
is a lot of hard work.

00:05:21.963 --> 00:05:24.376
- I actually spend a bunch of time

00:05:24.376 --> 00:05:26.714
analyzing and reflecting on why it was

00:05:26.714 --> 00:05:30.680
that we were even able
to do it because all,

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:32.066
like all reason suggests that we

00:05:32.066 --> 00:05:33.495
shouldn't have been able to do it, right,

00:05:33.495 --> 00:05:34.693
because all these other companies

00:05:34.693 --> 00:05:36.729
had way more engineering power,

00:05:36.729 --> 00:05:41.139
and servers and time and
money and all this stuff.

00:05:41.139 --> 00:05:43.961
And I actually think that this
is a pretty instructive thing

00:05:43.961 --> 00:05:45.483
for anything that you want to go do,

00:05:45.483 --> 00:05:47.786
because the same property
is going to be true

00:05:47.786 --> 00:05:49.235
for anything that you guys start,

00:05:49.235 --> 00:05:50.918
is that someone else is
going to have more resources

00:05:50.918 --> 00:05:51.810
and be able to do it.

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:53.428
The reason why I think
we actually ended up

00:05:53.428 --> 00:05:54.949
being the ones doing it is

00:05:54.949 --> 00:05:56.554
because we just cared way more about it

00:05:56.554 --> 00:05:57.851
than everyone else, right?

00:05:57.851 --> 00:05:59.714
So, there were always projects

00:05:59.714 --> 00:06:02.226
at some of these other companies
that were these hobbies,

00:06:02.226 --> 00:06:05.060
but we always thought that it was

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:06.165
this really important thing

00:06:06.165 --> 00:06:08.294
and really just like felt
in our gut, in our heart,

00:06:08.294 --> 00:06:10.310
that we wanted to do it.

00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:11.788
You know, early on there were always

00:06:11.788 --> 00:06:14.238
these skeptics saying that,
uh, this can't be a business,

00:06:14.238 --> 00:06:15.441
we didn't actually care that much about it

00:06:15.441 --> 00:06:17.061
being a business early on.

00:06:17.061 --> 00:06:19.420
But a lot of the reason
why bigger companies

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:20.814
didn't invest in it was because

00:06:20.814 --> 00:06:21.869
it wasn't clear that there was a model

00:06:21.869 --> 00:06:23.100
that would work for it.

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:24.231
- [Interviewer] It seemed like a bad idea.

00:06:24.231 --> 00:06:26.169
- Yeah, and I actually
think that that's true

00:06:26.169 --> 00:06:27.915
for a lot of the best ideas,

00:06:27.915 --> 00:06:30.306
where it is that it's not
that someone else can't do it,

00:06:30.306 --> 00:06:31.378
they actually can,

00:06:31.378 --> 00:06:33.087
and the odds are stacked against you,

00:06:33.087 --> 00:06:34.940
but I think often that belief in the fact

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:36.808
that you just care so much
about what you're doing

00:06:36.808 --> 00:06:39.892
is the only thing that kind
of drives you to do it.

00:06:39.892 --> 00:06:41.015
And, you know, to be honest,

00:06:41.015 --> 00:06:42.769
that kind of drives me
to this day, I mean,

00:06:42.769 --> 00:06:46.959
one of the big emphasis points
for the company right now

00:06:46.959 --> 00:06:48.707
is Internet.org, and you know,

00:06:48.707 --> 00:06:50.401
for awhile we had this rallying cry of,

00:06:50.401 --> 00:06:53.343
can we connect a billion
people and, you know,

00:06:53.343 --> 00:06:54.322
when we started talking about that,

00:06:54.322 --> 00:06:55.155
we thought that was crazy, right?

00:06:55.155 --> 00:06:57.590
It was way bigger than
any service in the world

00:06:57.590 --> 00:06:59.418
that had been built, and, you know,

00:06:59.418 --> 00:07:01.918
it was ten digits long, right,

00:07:03.515 --> 00:07:04.786
it felt crazy, we'd never get to that.

00:07:04.786 --> 00:07:06.415
But then, the thing is as we started

00:07:06.415 --> 00:07:08.268
to actually get closer to that,

00:07:08.268 --> 00:07:09.240
we took a step back and
we were like, alright,

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:10.492
well our mission isn't actually to get

00:07:10.492 --> 00:07:13.108
one in seven people in
the world to be connected,

00:07:13.108 --> 00:07:14.155
we want to connect everyone.

00:07:14.155 --> 00:07:17.107
So, it's a big issue that only
around a third of the people

00:07:17.107 --> 00:07:18.679
in the world have access to the internet,

00:07:18.679 --> 00:07:20.438
and that's something that we think

00:07:20.438 --> 00:07:21.477
that we can do something about,

00:07:21.477 --> 00:07:23.120
and similar to early Facebook,

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:25.170
there's no business model around this.

00:07:25.170 --> 00:07:27.388
I mean, all the people who have
all the money in the world,

00:07:27.388 --> 00:07:29.073
I mean it's not necessarily a fair thing,

00:07:29.073 --> 00:07:31.089
are already the people who
are on Facebook, right?

00:07:31.089 --> 00:07:33.633
It's in the first, you
know, seventh of the world,

00:07:33.633 --> 00:07:35.569
but we just believe really strongly,

00:07:35.569 --> 00:07:37.288
it's like this is what we are here to do,

00:07:37.288 --> 00:07:40.259
this is what our company
cares about, I care about it,

00:07:40.259 --> 00:07:42.284
the team cares about it,
our culture cares about it.

00:07:42.284 --> 00:07:44.297
So, we're just going to keep pushing on it

00:07:44.297 --> 00:07:45.523
and I actually think a lot of the reason

00:07:45.523 --> 00:07:47.108
why great stuff gets built

00:07:47.108 --> 00:07:49.483
is because it's kind of
irrational at the time.

00:07:49.483 --> 00:07:52.029
So, it kind of selects for the people

00:07:52.029 --> 00:07:55.029
who care the most about it doing it.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:04.112
- We are not a company that
normally China call on,

00:08:04.112 --> 00:08:06.897
we call ourselves a
company running a platform,

00:08:06.897 --> 00:08:10.763
a company that is running an ecosystem.

00:08:10.763 --> 00:08:11.977
We are almost everywhere,

00:08:11.977 --> 00:08:16.141
people say, Jack, you know you
are in the B to B and B to C

00:08:16.141 --> 00:08:17.421
and you're calling C to B

00:08:17.421 --> 00:08:20.895
on finance and online payment,
logistics, cloud computing,

00:08:20.895 --> 00:08:22.902
you are everywhere.

00:08:22.902 --> 00:08:25.363
Not because we're greedy,
not because we want to do it,

00:08:25.363 --> 00:08:26.518
because we have to do it.

00:08:26.518 --> 00:08:29.224
If we do not do it, China e-commerce

00:08:29.224 --> 00:08:31.474
in the years will collapse.

00:08:32.654 --> 00:08:36.712
I remember nine years ago
when I started Alipay.

00:08:36.712 --> 00:08:39.234
People said, Jack, don't
touch the financing sector,

00:08:39.234 --> 00:08:42.734
'cause it's illegal, you may be in prison.

00:08:44.451 --> 00:08:48.184
And we thought to ourselves,
if we don't do it,

00:08:48.184 --> 00:08:52.435
the China e-commerce will
always, like a chat room,

00:08:52.435 --> 00:08:54.772
everybody say, it's a good
thing and I don't pay,

00:08:54.772 --> 00:08:56.213
you don't get the things.

00:08:56.213 --> 00:08:58.684
So, I say, well, put me in the prison,

00:08:58.684 --> 00:09:02.763
let's make sure that Alipay,
the payment solution, works.

00:09:02.763 --> 00:09:05.893
And people said the payment
solution is such a stupid idea,

00:09:05.893 --> 00:09:07.514
it's an escrow service.

00:09:07.514 --> 00:09:09.683
I pay, you hold the money,
and you get the things,

00:09:09.683 --> 00:09:11.652
you pay the money, if you
don't get the things you want,

00:09:11.652 --> 00:09:13.452
return the money.

00:09:13.452 --> 00:09:16.837
They said it's a stupid
product, and I say,

00:09:16.837 --> 00:09:18.852
as long as it'll solve the problem,

00:09:18.852 --> 00:09:20.921
I like stupid things, you know.

00:09:20.921 --> 00:09:25.088
Let's make stupid things smart,
keep on improving every day.

00:09:30.978 --> 00:09:32.864
- Everything I did I
was following something

00:09:32.864 --> 00:09:35.376
I really cared about, something I loved,

00:09:35.376 --> 00:09:37.364
something I was passionate about.

00:09:37.364 --> 00:09:40.603
And I kept following that
passion, whether it was cars,

00:09:40.603 --> 00:09:42.668
whether it was anthropology,

00:09:42.668 --> 00:09:45.335
whether it was art, photography.

00:09:46.802 --> 00:09:50.666
And eventually it led
me to my huge passion,

00:09:50.666 --> 00:09:52.394
my real passion, which was making movies,

00:09:52.394 --> 00:09:54.117
which combined all of those things.

00:09:54.117 --> 00:09:55.865
And I realized that had I gone on

00:09:55.865 --> 00:09:58.096
to get my degree in Anthropology

00:09:58.096 --> 00:09:59.950
I would have probably made
anthropological movies

00:09:59.950 --> 00:10:00.868
in New Guinea or someplace

00:10:00.868 --> 00:10:03.563
and eventually been on
National Geographic and,

00:10:03.563 --> 00:10:06.847
you know, The History Channel,

00:10:06.847 --> 00:10:09.594
and then making features
and I had done Star Wars

00:10:09.594 --> 00:10:10.840
just the same.

00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:13.763
If I'd have gone to arts center,
and become an illustrator,

00:10:13.763 --> 00:10:15.506
I would have probably
started doing animation

00:10:15.506 --> 00:10:17.810
and doing animated films
and making animated things,

00:10:17.810 --> 00:10:18.889
and then ultimately I would have gone on

00:10:18.889 --> 00:10:20.179
and been right where I was.

00:10:20.179 --> 00:10:22.653
So, no matter which route I took,

00:10:22.653 --> 00:10:24.378
because I cared about all of them,

00:10:24.378 --> 00:10:26.780
they all led to the same place.

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:29.160
Had I done what my father
had wished me to do,

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:33.128
which is to go into the office
equipment business with him,

00:10:33.128 --> 00:10:35.805
which I knew I wasn't going
to do, I knew I hated that,

00:10:35.805 --> 00:10:39.050
my life would have been unpleasant.

00:10:39.050 --> 00:10:41.951
And, so I think it's very important

00:10:41.951 --> 00:10:45.784
not to do what your peers
think you should do,

00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:50.492
not do what your parents
think you should do,

00:10:50.492 --> 00:10:53.783
or your teachers, but
to do what you inside

00:10:53.783 --> 00:10:57.950
or even your culture thinks,
but do what's inside you.

00:11:02.630 --> 00:11:03.463
- Then what's your advice

00:11:03.463 --> 00:11:06.477
to these small business founders today?

00:11:06.477 --> 00:11:08.390
- Work hard, you're
going to need some luck,

00:11:08.390 --> 00:11:10.877
but the harder you work
the luckier you get.

00:11:10.877 --> 00:11:14.299
And if it doesn't work out,
that's not the end of the world.

00:11:14.299 --> 00:11:15.602
I just don't have anything in common

00:11:15.602 --> 00:11:17.456
with people that sit there and say,

00:11:17.456 --> 00:11:19.491
oh, my God, it was terrible, so,

00:11:19.491 --> 00:11:22.257
it's water over the dam, under the bridge,

00:11:22.257 --> 00:11:24.356
get on with it and start
another business the next day

00:11:24.356 --> 00:11:25.941
or another career.

00:11:25.941 --> 00:11:27.660
And be honest, look in a mirror

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:29.293
and say, why didn't it work out?

00:11:29.293 --> 00:11:31.872
Well, it wasn't the
skill set, or it wasn't,

00:11:31.872 --> 00:11:33.304
I didn't have the temperament for it,

00:11:33.304 --> 00:11:36.141
or I was in the wrong place, wrong time,

00:11:36.141 --> 00:11:38.106
maybe I can afford to do
it again, maybe I can't.

00:11:38.106 --> 00:11:40.768
But, people aren't honest with themselves.

00:11:40.768 --> 00:11:42.643
What I've always done is
I've taken a yellow pad,

00:11:42.643 --> 00:11:44.067
I draw a line down the middle,

00:11:44.067 --> 00:11:47.052
write out the pros on one
side, the cons on another,

00:11:47.052 --> 00:11:49.268
in the sense that if I let you see it

00:11:49.268 --> 00:11:50.920
you wouldn't laugh at me.

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:52.267
And, then when I get done,

00:11:52.267 --> 00:11:53.100
I just rip it up and throw it away

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:56.586
because I've worked out
in my mind what is real

00:11:56.586 --> 00:11:59.275
and what isn't and what
I can present to people

00:11:59.275 --> 00:12:00.692
and what I can't.

00:12:06.841 --> 00:12:10.124
- I mean, what made you single handedly

00:12:10.124 --> 00:12:13.541
decide to replenish America's bison herd?

00:12:17.566 --> 00:12:19.066
- Save everything.

00:12:20.778 --> 00:12:23.811
Remember, I'm supposed to
be shooting for something

00:12:23.811 --> 00:12:27.811
that's impossible to have
happen in my lifetime.

00:12:30.082 --> 00:12:31.881
- [Interviewer] And he
has done the impossible.

00:12:31.881 --> 00:12:35.497
Many times, like the 1979 Fastnet Race

00:12:35.497 --> 00:12:36.912
off the coast of England.

00:12:36.912 --> 00:12:39.836
A massive storm whipped up winds and waves

00:12:39.836 --> 00:12:42.429
that left 15 sailors dead.

00:12:42.429 --> 00:12:45.622
To this day, it's considered
the most dangerous ocean race,

00:12:45.622 --> 00:12:49.574
ever, and Ted didn't just survive, he won.

00:12:49.574 --> 00:12:52.230
And that's where we
started our conversation

00:12:52.230 --> 00:12:54.147
about his amazing life.

00:12:55.001 --> 00:12:56.345
Was there ever a moment that you thought

00:12:56.345 --> 00:12:58.135
you wouldn't make it?

00:12:58.135 --> 00:13:00.811
- No, but I didn't spend
a whole lot of time

00:13:00.811 --> 00:13:03.014
thinking about not making it.

00:13:03.014 --> 00:13:05.326
I was trying to figure
out how to keep going.

00:13:05.326 --> 00:13:06.648
- Where you afraid?

00:13:06.648 --> 00:13:07.574
- No.

00:13:07.574 --> 00:13:10.979
- Did you ever think of not
going through with the race?

00:13:10.979 --> 00:13:11.812
Was it ever-?

00:13:11.812 --> 00:13:12.733
- Dropping out?

00:13:12.733 --> 00:13:13.983
Hahaha, no way.

00:13:15.765 --> 00:13:19.765
That would have been like
dropping out with CNN.

00:13:24.313 --> 00:13:28.039
- My goal is to simplify complexity.

00:13:28.039 --> 00:13:29.578
I just want to build stuff

00:13:29.578 --> 00:13:32.915
that really simplifies our
base human interaction.

00:13:32.915 --> 00:13:35.104
Twitter was around communication

00:13:35.104 --> 00:13:38.315
and visualizing what was happening
in the world in realtime.

00:13:38.315 --> 00:13:41.878
Square was allowing everyone
to accept the form of payment

00:13:41.878 --> 00:13:43.614
that people have in their pocket today,

00:13:43.614 --> 00:13:45.101
which is a credit card.

00:13:45.101 --> 00:13:48.824
So, we've built the simplest
way to accept credit cards

00:13:48.824 --> 00:13:51.253
and it's just this little, tiny device

00:13:51.253 --> 00:13:52.773
that we give away for free,

00:13:52.773 --> 00:13:55.510
and you just download some
software from the app store

00:13:55.510 --> 00:13:58.335
and you plug it in to your
mobile phone or your iPad.

00:13:58.335 --> 00:14:00.503
Then, suddenly, you can take credit cards,

00:14:00.503 --> 00:14:03.853
so if you're a tax accountant,
or a lawyer, or a doctor,

00:14:03.853 --> 00:14:08.582
or even a hair stylist, you
can now accept credit cards.

00:14:08.582 --> 00:14:09.519
- Thank you guys so much for watching!

00:14:09.519 --> 00:14:10.950
I'd love to know, what did you think?

00:14:10.950 --> 00:14:13.117
Did you enjoy this video, did
you take something from it?

00:14:13.117 --> 00:14:14.713
What was your favorite point,

00:14:14.713 --> 00:14:16.851
do you want to see the
series continue or not

00:14:16.851 --> 00:14:18.715
since this is a brand new test for me.

00:14:18.715 --> 00:14:20.506
Let me know, leave it down
in the comments below.

00:14:20.506 --> 00:14:23.663
Thank you guys so much for
watching, I believe in you.

00:14:23.663 --> 00:14:25.726
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself,

00:14:25.726 --> 00:14:29.256
and whatever your one word is, much love.

00:14:29.256 --> 00:14:30.620
I'll see you soon.

